************** G E N E R A L   I N F O R M A T I O N *************4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
This evaluated data file is based on a software system built      4834 1451
around the nuclear model code TALYS [kon07], version 1.74. It is  4834 1451
part of a large collection of isotopic evaluations, all created by4834 1451
running the system with either adjusted or default input          4834 1451
parameters. This means that the data in the entire collection have4834 1451
been tested in detail for many isotopes against individual        4834 1451
experimental data, while for other isotopes it is only as good as 4834 1451
the global quality of TALYS at the moment. The mutual quality of  4834 1451
all individual data files is however consistent: The same set of  4834 1451
nuclear models is used and, equally important, the same ENDF-6    4834 1451
formatting procedures for each isotope. The data file has been    4834 1451
created automatically using the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.    4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
The resulting data file provides a complete representation of     4834 1451
nuclear data needed for transport, activation, damage, heating,   4834 1451
radioactivity, and shielding applications over the incident       4834 1451
neutron energy range from 1.e-11 to 200. MeV.                     4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
All transport and activation data for neutrons, charged particles,4834 1451
photons and residual nuclides are filed. This includes cross      4834 1451
sections, angular distributions, double-differential spectra,     4834 1451
isomeric production, discrete and continuum photon production     4834 1451
cross sections, residual production cross sections and recoils.   4834 1451
This general purpose evaluation can thus be used as both          4834 1451
transport and activation library.                                 4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
A complete set of covariance data is included, which is stored    4834 1451
in MF31-40.                                                       4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
**** N U C L I D E    S P E C I F I C   I N F O R M A T I O N ****4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
                      Resonance Range                             4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
  --------------------------------------------------------------- 4834 1451
  --------------------------------------------------------------- 4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
  General methodology: The global approach considered in this     4834 1451
          work is presented in the following paper: Modern        4834 1451
          nuclear data evaluation with the TALYS code system,     4834 1451
          A.J. Koning and D. Rochman, Nuclear Data Sheets 113     4834 1451
          (2012) 2841.                                            4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
  MF2:  Resolved resonance range  (RRR)                           4834 1451
       The RRR was generated with TARES-1.31, compiled on         4834 1451
       12-10-2015 11:58:11 PM at Villingen  , Switzerland. The RRR4834 1451
       expands from 0 to 6.088055E+2 eV. A total of               4834 1451
       2 l-values are used and 200 resonances. The resonance      4834 1451
       formalism is multi-level Breit Wigner (MLBW).              4834 1451
       The ladder approach from the CALENDF code is used to       4834 1451
       generate statistical resonances in the unresolved          4834 1451
       resonance range. Therefore, the URR is translated into     4834 1451
       resolved resonance range. Explanations about the method    4834 1451
       can be found in the CALENDF manual: CEA-Report CEA-R-6277  4834 1451
       "CALENDF-2010: User manual" by J.C. Sublet, P. Ribon and   4834 1451
       M. Coste-Delcaux.                                          4834 1451
       The method of creating statistical resonances in the       4834 1451
       URR region is described in: "From average parameters to    4834 1451
       statistical resolved resonances", D. Rochman et al.,       4834 1451
       Annals of Nucl. Ene. 51 (2013) 60.                         4834 1451
       The s-wave average level spacing is 3.8 eV and             4834 1451
       the s-wave neutron strength is 6.134e-05 1e-4.             4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
       After the ladder method, the retroactive method is applied 4834 1451
       to update the MF32 and MF2 with the SAMMY code.            4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
  MF32: Covariance file for resonance parameters                  4834 1451
        The compact format is used to represent the covariance    4834 1451
        information on the resonance parameters. Uncertainties    4834 1451
        come from compilations, EXFOR or existing libraries and   4834 1451
        correlations between parameters are obtained following    4834 1451
        the method presented in NIM/A 589 (2008) 85.              4834 1451
        SAMMY is used in the retroactive mode to update MF32.     4834 1451
                                                                  4834 1451
